.TH BUBECTL 1 "October 2026" "Bubernetes" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bubectl \- control a Bubernetes cluster
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bubectl
.RI [ options ]
.B apply \-f
.I FILE
.br
.B bubectl
.RI [ options ]
.B get
.RB [ nodes | deploy | static | all ]
.br
.B bubectl
.RI [ options ]
.B delete
.IR NAME | Kind/NAME
.br
.B bubectl
.RI [ options ]
.B dump
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bubectl
talks to one node of a Bubernetes cluster over the same wire protocol the nodes
use among themselves. Any node will do: every node holds the whole desired
state, and a change made through one reaches the others by gossip. See
.BR bubernetes (7)
for the model.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "apply \-f " FILE
Define or update the resources described in
.IR FILE ,
a manifest of one or more documents. For each document whose
.B image:
is a path,
.B bubectl
reads the executable and sends its bytes along with the specification, so the
cluster holds the image from then on; the path need not exist on any node.
Applying a document whose resource already exists with the same specification
changes nothing.
.TP
.BR "get " [ nodes | deploy | static | all ]
List resources. With no argument, workloads (Static and Deploy);
.B nodes
lists members with their state and address. Columns:
.B REPL
is the desired replica count
.RB ( all
for a daemonset),
.B STATUS
and
.B RUNNING
are the answering node's own view (how many replicas
.I it
runs), and
.B NODE/ORIGIN
is the member address, or for a workload the node that last edited it and the
version.
.TP
.BI "delete " NAME
Retract a workload from the whole cluster: its replicas are stopped everywhere
and a tombstone prevents it coming back. If both a Static and a Deploy carry
the name, give
.IR Kind/NAME ,
e.g.
.BR Deploy/web .
A resource defined by an edit file in a node's manifest directory comes back
if that file is edited again; remove the file to delete it for good.
.TP
.B dump
Print the cluster's whole desired state as a manifest of state documents
(with
.B version:
and
.BR origin: ).
Redirect it to a file to take a backup; place that file in a fresh node's
manifest directory to restore the cluster's view there.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may appear anywhere on the command line.
.TP
.BI \-\-server " IP" [ :PORT ]
The node to talk to. The port defaults to 7700.
.TP
.BI \-\-certs " DIR"
Present the client certificate in
.I DIR
(the same layout as
.BR bubelet (8)'s
.BR \-\-certs )
to a cluster built with TLS. Required for such a cluster; harmless otherwise.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The server is read, in rising priority, from
.IR ~/.bube/config ,
.IR ./.bube/config ,
the environment variable
.BR BUBE_SERVER ,
and
.BR \-\-server .
The file holds a single line:
.PP
.nf
server: 10.0.0.7:7700
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 if the node could not be reached or the request was refused,
2 on a usage error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
$ bubectl --server 10.0.0.7 get nodes
NAME       KIND   REPL  STATUS  RUNNING  NODE/ORIGIN
node-a     Node   -     Ready   -        10.0.0.7:7700
node-b     Node   -     Ready   -        10.0.0.161:7700

$ cat web.yaml
kind: Deploy
name: web
replicas: -1
image: /usr/local/bin/nginx
argv: [-g, "daemon off;"]

$ bubectl apply -f web.yaml
applied Deploy 'web' (replicas=-1)

$ bubectl get
NAME   KIND    REPL  STATUS  RUNNING  NODE/ORIGIN
web    Deploy  all   Ready   1        @node-a v2

$ bubectl dump > backup.yaml
$ bubectl delete web
deleted 'web'
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bubernetes (7),
.BR bubelet (8)
